                  "rawText": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Round 2 candidate (candidate-02) for MIS_MTZ7SYVM: replaces candidate-01's rejected direction (pure in-place op fusion, 11.264us, still launch-bound) with CUDA-graph capture/replay of the fused affine+relu chain inside run(inputs), so the steady-state call collapses to a single graph launch instead of 4 eager kernel launches. Input generation, test cases, benchmark profiles and reference() are byte-identical to the baseline.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"Latency is dominated by per-kernel launch/host overhead, not by arithmetic: 32x256 FP32 has ~8K elements, so each of the eager chain's kernels costs ~2.4-2.8us of launch/sync time. Baseline (6 kernels incl. the redundant clone) = 14.336us; candidate-01 (4 kernels, in-place) = 11.264us, i.e. ~2.8us saved per removed kernel. Removing kernels one at a time cannot cross the gate, so the remaining lever is collapsing the whole chain into a single replayable launch.\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"kernel launch / host-side dispatch overhead (~2.5-2.8us per launch) dominates a tiny 32x256 elementwise workload\",\"eager PyTorch cannot fuse scalar-scale+shift, so the affine+relu chain needs a minimum of 4 separate kernels\",\"Python-level dispatch cost per op adds to the per-launch overhead in the timed path\"]},\"patch\":\"diff --git a/run.py b/run.py\\n--- a/run.py\\n+++ b/run.py\\n@@ -22,6 +22,71 @@ def reference(inputs):\\n     return torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n \\n-def run(inputs):\\n-    # Initial implementation deliberately does one redundant device copy.\\n-    value = torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n-    return value.clone()\\n+def _affine_relu(x, y):\\n+    # 1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125 -> relu, using in-place writes on one buffer.\\n+    out = torch.mul(x, 1.25)\\n+    out.add_(y, alpha=0.75)\\n+    out.sub_(0.125)\\n+    return out.relu_()\\n+\\n+\\n+_GRAPH_LIMIT = 8\\n+_graphs = {}\\n+\\n+\\n+def _key(t):\\n+    return (t.data_ptr(), tuple(t.shape), t.dtype, t.device.index)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _capture(x, y):\\n+    # Warm the allocation/launch path on a side stream, then capture the chain.\\n+    side = torch.cuda.Stream()\\n+    side.wait_stream(torch.cuda.current_stream())\\n+    with torch.cuda.stream(side):\\n+        for _ in range(3):\\n+            _affine_relu(x, y)\\n+    torch.cuda.current_stream().wait_stream(side)\\n+    torch.cuda.synchronize()\\n+    graph = torch.cuda.CUDAGraph()\\n+    with torch.cuda.graph(graph):\\n+        out = _affine_relu(x, y)\\n+    graph.replay()\\n+    return graph, out\\n+\\n+\\n+def run(inputs):\\n+    x, y = inputs['x'], inputs['y']\\n+    cuda_ok = (\\n+        x.is_cuda and y.is_cuda and x.shape == y.shape\\n+        and hasattr(torch.cuda, 'CUDAGraph')\\n+        and torch.cuda.is_available()\\n+        and not torch.cuda.is_current_stream_capturing()\\n+    )\\n+    if not cuda_ok:\\n+        return _affine_relu(x, y)\\n+\\n+    key = (_key(x), _key(y))\\n+    entry = _graphs.get(key)\\n+    if entry is None:\\n+        if len(_graphs) < _GRAPH_LIMIT:\\n+            # Hold strong refs so the captured input pointers stay live.\\n+            _graphs[key] = {'x': x, 'y': y, 'graph': None, 'out': None}\\n+        return _affine_relu(x, y)\\n+\\n+    if entry['graph'] is None:\\n+        try:\\n+            entry['graph'], entry['out'] = _capture(entry['x'], entry['y'])\\n+        except Exception:\\n+            _graphs.pop(key, None)\\n+            return _affine_relu(x, y)\\n+\\n+    # Same input buffers seen again: replay the captured chain in one launch.\\n+    entry['graph'].replay()\\n+    return entry['out']\\n\",\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-02\",\"title\":\"Capture the fused affine+relu chain in a CUDA graph and replay it in one launch\",\"hypothesis\":\"Since the 32x256 workload is launch-bound (~2.5-2.8us per kernel launch, evidenced by baseline 14.336us/6 kernels and candidate-01 11.264us/4 kernels), collapsing the remaining 4-kernel chain into a single CUDA graph replay removes ~4 launch overheads per call and should cut steady-state p50 well below the 11.264us reachable by op-level fusion alone.\",\"change\":\"run(inputs) now: (a) on a first-seen (data_ptr, shape, dtype, device) input pair, executes the fused in-place chain eagerly and registers the buffers; (b) on the next call with the same buffers (benchmark warmup/repeats), warms up on a side stream and captures the chain with torch.cuda.CUDAGraph, then replays it and returns the graph-owned output; (c) on any capture failure or non-CUDA/shape-mismatch input, falls back to the same eager fused chain. Cache is bounded to 8 entries holding strong tensor references so captured pointers stay valid; the graph is only captured when the same buffers recur, so one-shot correctness calls never capture. Input generation, test cases, benchmark profiles and reference() are unchanged.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[],\"risks\":[\"CUDA graph capture can be rejected in some runner configurations; guarded by try/except with eager fallback, but a failed capture mid-way could disturb stream state\",\"Returning the graph-owned output buffer means successive replays reuse the same tensor object; safe if the harness compares each result before the next run() call, which the per-case correctness flow implies\",\"If the harness passes freshly allocated inputs on every timed iteration, keys never repeat and the candidate degrades to candidate-01-level eager performance (~11.26us) rather than improving\",\"Graph private memory pools are retained for up to 8 shapes (~ms-level capture cost per shape, paid during warmup only)\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-02\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Queue candidate-02 for shared-GPU validation\",\"reason\":\"This is a bounded, single-file, semantically neutral change that attacks the measured bottleneck (launch overhead) by a different mechanism than the rejected candidate-01, and it degrades gracefully to the candidate-01 eager path if capture is unavailable or inputs are not reused.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"4/4 correctness on primary and small profiles plus a p50 measurement for the primary 32x256 profile; compare against the 14.336us baseline and the 11.264us candidate-01 reference.\",\"risk\":\"medium\"},\"risks\":[\"Performance target (>=99.9999% latency reduction) is far beyond what launch-overhead elimination on an 8K-element elementwise op can deliver; even a perfect single-launch implementation is bounded by ~3-5us of launch/measurement floor\",\"CUDA graph capture depends on runner permissions and on the benchmark harness reusing input buffers across warmup/repeats\",\"No local execution was permitted, so capture behavior and pointer stability could not be verified before queueing\"]}"
                },
